CARLA ue58-dev: the client light API changes the light, not only its record.

UCarlaLight::SetLightState (set_intensity, set_color, set_light_state, turn_on/off from a client) set the
CarlaLight's intensity and colour and fired the blueprint UpdateLights event, whose graphs in the ported lamp
blueprints never reach the light components, so a lamp kept its Light Defaults (BP_StreetLight06_A: 27,000 lm, warm
white) whatever a client asked; only on/off worked (ApplyLightOnToComponents). This applies intensity and colour
natively, as SetLightIntensity/SetLightColor (used by the Light Defaults) already do. Coupled CARLA needs it for the
register lamps' colour temperature and lumens (coupledcarla/nightlights.py).

Apply in a CARLA ue58-dev tree (checked against 38b72cc52): git apply <this file>; then rebuild the editor
(lin5080: ~/build_editor58.sh, an incremental build of one changed file in the Carla module: estimated 5-15 min) and,
for the packaged server, the package (win5090 task cc58-package; content is unchanged, so mostly compile and stage,
estimated under an hour). Not posted upstream.

--- a/Unreal/CarlaUnreal/Plugins/Carla/Source/Carla/Lights/CarlaLight.cpp
+++ b/Unreal/CarlaUnreal/Plugins/Carla/Source/Carla/Lights/CarlaLight.cpp
@@ -590,6 +590,17 @@
   flags = bOn ? (flags | ECarlaLightFlags::TurnedOn) : (flags & ~ECarlaLightFlags::TurnedOn);
   UpdateLights();
   ApplyLightOnToComponents(bOn);
+  // The client light API (set_intensity, set_color, set_light_state) arrives
+  // here. UpdateLights() is the blueprint event that should push the new
+  // intensity and colour into the light components, but the ported lamp
+  // blueprints' UpdateLights graphs never reach them (see
+  // ApplyIntensityToComponents), so the client changed only this record:
+  // get_intensity() answered the new value while the lamp kept shining with
+  // its Light Defaults. Push both natively, as SetLightIntensity and
+  // SetLightColor do. ApplyIntensityToComponents records what it wrote, so
+  // the legacy conversion below leaves it alone.
+  ApplyIntensityToComponents(Intensity);
+  ApplyColorToComponents(Color);
   ApplyLegacyComponentConversion();
   RecordLightChange();
 }
